0,Ethnic origin of Muong in Vietnam,https://eastasiaorigin.blogspot.com/2018/11/ethnic-origin-of-muong.html,Ethnic Muong are part of Austroasiatic tribes who originated in central Laos and central Vietnam before migrating to northeastern Vietnam. They belong to the South-East Asian group with darker skin than the ethnic Kinh.,Muong,Austro-Asiatic
1,Austro-Asiatic etymology : List with all references,https://starlingdb.org/cgi-bin/etymology.cgi?single=1&basename=/data/aas/aaet&text_number=+397&root=config,"Presyllable: kǝN-. Austro-Asiatic etymology: Austro-Asiatic etymology. Modern Mon: khamau, camhau, samhau.Meaning: mouth. Borean etymology: Borean etymology. Proto-Austroasiatic: *bʔɔh.",Muong,Austro-Asiatic
2,Languages : Austroasiatic Family,https://krysstal.com/langfams_austroasia.html,The Austro-Asiatic Family are a scattered group of languages in Asia. They are found from eastern India to Vietnam.The Viet-Muong Branch includes Vietnamese and Muong (both languages of Vietnam). The former is written in a form of the Latin script.,Muong,Austro-Asiatic
3,Austro-Asiatic languages - Academic Kids,https://academickids.com/encyclopedia/Austroasiatic_language,Austro-Asiatic languages. The Austroasiatic languages are a large language family of Southeast Asia and India. The name comes from the Greek word for South Asia.,Muong,Austro-Asiatic
4,"Austroasiatic languages | Mon-Khmer, Munda & Vietic | Britannica",https://www.britannica.com/topic/Austroasiatic-languages,"Austroasiatic languages, stock of some 150 languages spoken by more than 65 million people scattered throughout Southeast Asia and eastern India. Most of these languages have numerous dialects.",Muong,Austro-Asiatic
5,WALS Online - Family Austro-Asiatic,https://wals.info/languoid/family/austroasiatic,Family Austro-Asiatic. Legend. Aslian.Muong. Vietnamese.,Muong,Austro-Asiatic
6,Austroasiatic language spoken in Vietnam - Wikidata,https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3236789,No label defined. – English. Muong. Austroasiatic language spoken in Vietnam. Muong language.,Muong,Austro-Asiatic
